/// @defgroup gfx gfx
/// Drawing with SDL_GPU: the device, the swapchain, pipelines and frames. Each feature keeps its
/// pipelines and buffers in its own class under `internal/features/` (sprites, meshes, ribbons, seals, the world map's
/// ground and rivers, the composite); the
/// seals are laid out on the CPU (seal_draw.hpp) and drawn by the seal shaders, each layer's parts as distance fields, and
/// their light (pillars, sparks and shards) placed in the light's vertex shader from one record a seal (SealParticleAt(),
/// mirrored in C++ for the tests), with no particle state: the ground ones over the ground and decals, the upright and
/// overhead ones over the creatures but under the swords' trails, the overlay, text and the HUD.
///
/// @par Draw order
/// The world pass draws, in this order (a new layer goes in this list; each layer's detail is in the header named):
/// -# The painted ground, when the snapshot shows the world map (`Snapshot::worldMap`: only the world scene, which
///    publishes no tiles and no painted ground): `gfx/ground.hpp`, `gfx/internal/features/ground.hpp`.
/// -# The rivers on it, then the roads over them, shaded by the fog as land is: `gfx/river_draw.hpp`,
///    `gfx/road_draw.hpp`, `gfx/internal/features/line_ribbons.hpp`.
/// -# The terrain's sprites (bridges, fords, stamps, place paintings, mist puffs), then the ground pipeline again in its
///    veil mode, then the place markers: `gfx/terrain_draw.hpp`, `gfx/stamp_draw.hpp`, `gfx/tile_hash.hpp`,
///    `Renderer::SetWorld`.
/// -# The player's route: `gfx/route_draw.hpp`.
/// -# The snapshot's ground and decals (its first `Snapshot::groundBatches`), then the ground seals (washes and ink with
///    normal blend, then lines and light additive): `gfx/seal_draw.hpp`, `gfx/internal/features/seals.hpp`.
/// -# The rest of the snapshot's sprites, then the selection and hover outlines (the glows `selected` and `hover`).
/// -# The ground pipeline in its cloud-shade mode while the view has fog, so nothing on unexplored land shows through the
///    clouds' gaps (only the debug sight draws walkers there).
/// -# The fog's clouds (`gfx/cloud_draw.hpp`), then the target cursor's tile (`gfx/route_draw.hpp`).
/// -# The upright and overhead seals, light included, capped in opacity and under all that follows, so the swords fly out
///    over them and health bars, numbers and the HUD stay readable.
/// -# The swords' trails (additive ribbons).
/// -# The overlay the main thread places each frame at the render time: swords wearing their glow, the poison lance's
///    bursts, then the health bars (`gfx/volley_draw.hpp`).
/// -# Text, then the HUD in target pixels, its bars through the overlay's sprites and then its lines; on the world map
///    the date line, the HUD's one clickable area (`app/hud.hpp`).
///
/// The fog is no step of its own: its texture (`View::fog` through `internal::FogTexture`) is bound to every draw of
/// the world pass, up to the overlay and the HUD, which shade by it as the instance flags in `core/frame.hpp` say
/// (`gfx/fog.hpp`, `gfx/internal/features/sprites.hpp`); labels fade by `FogOpacity` (`gfx/labels.hpp`) as they are
/// placed.
